  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$",
